
# Host q's own check at second 31 is still running in the reference's
# recording when c2's DOWN result comes, 2 ms later, so the on-demand
# check of q that c2's change asks for does nothing. In a real run of
# checkd q's check may end a millisecond before c2's result; the on-demand
# check then finds a fresh result, moves q's check to now, and passes it
# over until the result is 15 s old: q is checked again at 47. The
# reference does the same when the order is that one (fresh-parent). A
# real run cannot be held to the millisecond.
ignore-real q a 1 ms race at second 31 decides whether q is checked again at 47; see fresh-parent
